 "why": "Two checks on the table's ionization energies, plus its first look at negative ions. (1) Every cation (Z − 1 electrons) searched for its own ground configuration, not only the bake's 'take one electron out of the atom's own configuration'. If an ion rearranges, its ionization energy drops; element 128's 29.6 eV is the one to test. (2) Every anion (Z + 1), for electron affinities. A neutral 4D atom pulls on a distant electron as −Σ⟨r²⟩/8s³, so atoms might hold an extra electron where ours don't. Candidates count only if every occupied orbital is bound. Both worlds: ours (1–36) is the check against measured ionizations and affinities. termtable.mjs with a charge argument: the same solver, the neutral configurations around each ion moved to its electron count as seeds, the Hund correction applied.",
